1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,602
Konnichiwa. This is Liam, in for Bear, with a short film for INFO 7375,

2
00:00:04,622 --> 00:00:08,563
Computational Skepticism for AI. You might think a skeptic should throw out

3
00:00:08,583 --> 00:00:13,185
induction, since the past can't prove the future. Don't. Induction is one of the

4
00:00:13,205 --> 00:00:17,146
most useful tools you have. The real question is when it breaks.

5
00:00:17,216 --> 00:00:21,776
Three terms. Induction: expecting the future to resemble the past. Stationary: a

6
00:00:21,796 --> 00:00:26,355
process whose statistical rules don't change over time. And ergodic: one long

7
00:00:26,375 --> 00:00:28,645
history speaks for the whole system.

8
00:00:28,715 --> 00:00:32,858
The move comes from David Hume, in An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding, from

9
00:00:32,878 --> 00:00:37,342
seventeen forty-eight. The sun has risen every morning of your life. Does that prove

10
00:00:37,362 --> 00:00:42,146
it will rise tomorrow? Hume said no. That the sun won't rise tomorrow is no

11
00:00:42,166 --> 00:00:45,669
contradiction. You can picture it. The past gives no logical guarantee.

12
00:00:45,739 --> 00:00:50,379
But Hume didn't say stop. Only a fool or a madman, he wrote, would dispute the

13
00:00:50,399 --> 00:00:54,456
authority of experience. We can't prove the future will resemble the past, and we

14
00:00:54,476 --> 00:00:58,533
live by it anyway. He called that custom: the great guide of human life.

15
00:00:58,603 --> 00:01:02,842
And most of the time, induction works. For ten years, online shopping's share of

16
00:01:02,863 --> 00:01:07,102
U.S. retail sales climbed slowly and steadily: from about four percent in twenty ten

17
00:01:07,122 --> 00:01:11,666
to about eleven percent at the end of twenty nineteen, per the U.S. Census Bureau.

18
00:01:11,686 --> 00:01:15,621
Extend the line one more quarter, and you'd land close, quarter after quarter.

19
00:01:15,691 --> 00:01:20,260
Notice what held. Not the number: it kept rising. The rules held. Each quarter added

20
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:24,849
a small step, never more than half a point. A process whose statistical rules don't

21
00:01:24,869 --> 00:01:29,437
change over time is called stationary. These steps were close to it, and that's what

22
00:01:29,457 --> 00:01:30,661
induction quietly relies on.

23
00:01:30,731 --> 00:01:35,220
Ergodic is the other half. In an ergodic system, following one shop over a long

24
00:01:35,240 --> 00:01:39,730
stretch of time gives the same average as looking across all the shops at once.

25
00:01:39,750 --> 00:01:42,736
That's what lets one history stand in for the whole.

26
00:01:42,806 --> 00:01:47,457
Then, in the spring of twenty twenty, COVID changed how people buy. Stores closed or

27
00:01:47,477 --> 00:01:51,816
cut back, and shopping moved online. In a single quarter, online's share jumped from

28
00:01:51,836 --> 00:01:56,176
about twelve percent to about sixteen, per the Census Bureau. A decade of small

29
00:01:56,196 --> 00:01:57,733
steps, then one giant one.

30
00:01:57,803 --> 00:02:01,864
Nothing was wrong with the math. The system that made the history had changed. Hume

31
00:02:01,884 --> 00:02:06,217
saw that nothing forbids it: the course of nature may change, he wrote, and the past

32
00:02:06,237 --> 00:02:08,122
may be no rule for the future.

33
00:02:08,192 --> 00:02:13,402
A model trained on the old history makes the same bet. The chapter calls it a system

34
00:02:13,422 --> 00:02:17,709
trained on a stationary past, shipped into a moving present. After the change, the

35
00:02:17,729 --> 00:02:21,093
model is exactly as confident as it was the day before.

36
00:02:21,163 --> 00:02:25,687
So keep induction. Most of the time, it's the right bet. The honest rule: trust the

37
00:02:25,707 --> 00:02:29,948
pattern while the system that made it stays the same. When the world changes, go

38
00:02:29,968 --> 00:02:30,800
back and check.

39
00:02:30,870 --> 00:02:34,827
Your turn. Pick one pattern your team or your model relies on, and paste this into

40
00:02:34,847 --> 00:02:38,804
Claude: Here is a pattern my team or model relies on: describe it. What process in

41
00:02:38,824 --> 00:02:42,532
the world produces this pattern? What would have to change in that process for the

42
00:02:42,552 --> 00:02:46,261
pattern to stop holding? For each change, name one signal I could watch that would

43
00:02:46,281 --> 00:02:49,741
tell me it had happened. Then check two things yourself. Could each change really

44
00:02:49,761 --> 00:02:52,475
happen? And could you actually watch each signal, starting this week?

45
00:02:52,545 --> 00:02:57,550
Induction Works Until the World Changes. At Nik Bear Brown.
